2026-07-20 feat/team-metric-directions — set `direction` on the 5 team metrics currently classified `neutral` in metric_catalogue.csv (passes_per_match, duels_per_match, defensive_actions_per_match, corner_kicks_per_match, corners_against_per_match). `direction` is a §10 metric-meaning field, so the classification is recorded here for the audit trail (mirrors the #530b / #636 log-then-review pattern).
      CONTEXT: the v2 team Stats "vs the league" verdict view colours each metric bar green when the team beats the league median and ink when below. The CPO ruled this view must not classify any displayed metric `neutral` ("we shouldn't allow neutral classification. It has to be clearly defined as better or worse") and chose (AskUserQuestion, 2026-07-20) **"Give each a direction"** over the alternative (drop the style metrics from the view). The CPO then directed "define the direction in the metric layer first" + "adjust the fixture bars accordingly."
      DIRECTION = passes / duels / defensive-actions / corners -> **higher_better**; corners-against -> **lower_better**. Chosen to ALIGN with the existing `lower_is_better` boolean (false -> higher_better, true -> lower_better): that boolean (which the LIVE MVP already reads) has classified these all along, so the change only drops the v2 `neutral` abstention and makes the richer `direction` field consistent. `lower_is_better` is NOT touched.
      FOOTBALL-HONESTY OVERRIDE (CPO-accepted; flagged for the football-analytics-expert-reviewer): higher_better on duels_per_match + defensive_actions_per_match is a VOLUME signal, not a quality one — a possession-dominant side that duels/defends less reads "below par" despite being strong (exactly why the catalogue authors chose `neutral`). Unlike the #600/#530b goals rulings (a genuine outcome direction the reviewer confirmed football-sound), this is display clarity chosen over the abstention. The CPO weighed it twice in-conversation (the abstract "no defensible direction" flag before the AskUserQuestion, then the concrete possession-team consequence after) and chose the clear verdict; the interpretation text keeps the volume caveat honest. This is a fresh §10 CPO choice (NOT a settled-ruling inheritance), recorded so the reviewer's expected objection is documented, not re-litigated. This entry is the durable record the contract's decisions_taken (direction) rests on.
      REVIEW RESOLUTION (2026-07-20, football-analytics-expert-reviewer ESCALATE): the reviewer raised 4 questions — (1) the override quote named only duels/defensive-actions, not passes/corners; (2) the 2026-06-28 correlation sweep found defensive-action counts are noise vs final league rank; (3) defensive_actions is higher_better while its T/I/B atoms stay neutral; (4) metrics_display.md allegedly locks these as non-verdict. (1)+(2) were surfaced to the CPO as NEW information. CPO ANSWER (AskUserQuestion, 2026-07-20): **"Badge all five as decided"** — proceed with all 5 team metrics directional (green/ink on every metric), explicitly covering passes + corners, accepting the defensive-actions "noise vs rank" limitation as a display-clarity call over the data. PREMISE CORRECTION on question (4): close reading of metrics_display.md shows NO team conflict — the team display table (metrics_display.md:99-116) has no direction column, and the "neutral = no colour verdict" rule (metrics_display.md:66-91) is scoped to the PLAYER percentile bar and its PLAYER metrics (which STAY neutral); the "context row" (Ø Duels before % Duels won) is a layout adjacency, not a verdict. The reviewer conflated the player-side rule + the layout concept with the team change. So the locked doc needs NO edit and is not stale. Question (3): the T/I/B atoms are "defined but not displayed" (metrics_display.md:120-123, sub-display of row 10 only), carry no verdict bar, and stay `neutral` (changing them would exceed the CPO's "five displayed metrics"). ESCALATE resolved; committing the 5-metric change as reviewed, no doc edit.
      INTERPRETATION SWEEP (2026-07-20, CPO-directed, same PR): after the direction-is-judgement principle was stated ([[feedback-metric-direction-judgement]]: direction = "all else equal, is more better?", not a rank/results correlation), the CPO asked to review ALL interpretation text and adjust where applicable. Revised 3 team volume interpretations (shots_per_match, passes_per_match, duels_per_match) to LEAD with the direction reading and keep the style/volume caveat second; the other team interpretations already led with the judgement and were kept; neutral metrics (T/I/B atoms, sot_rank_gap, contribution_share, deferred player per-90 volumes) keep their no-verdict wording, consistent with their neutral direction. No direction flips. Interpretation wording is a display/narrative-seed text call the CPO directed explicitly; recorded for trail completeness. (Total interpretations reworded vs main = 6: shots/duels/defensive_actions/passes/corners/corners_against; the sweep this iteration touched shots/passes/duels, the other 3 were reworded with their direction in the first commit.)
      FOOTBALL RE-REVIEW (2026-07-20): the football-analytics-expert-reviewer PASSED the directions but FAILed 2 interpretation wordings as OVERCLAIMS: duels_per_match "a proactive front-foot side" (a side under sustained pressure also racks up duels) and passes_per_match "more control of the ball" (raw passes_total includes misplaced attempts; also collides with pass_accuracy's control framing). Both wording-only, not direction (decisions_reserved delegated wording to the reviewer). FIXED per its guidance, mirroring the honest defensive_actions caveat: duels -> "high = a physically engaged side (volume; can also reflect a side under sustained pressure)"; passes -> "high = sees more of the ball; low = a more direct style (accuracy shows how securely it is kept)". Re-ran the reviewer to confirm PASS.

2026-07-22 chore/guardrails-cover-design-surface — §11 REVERSED: an escalation must now END WITH A RECOMMENDATION. A §10 rule extension, escalated because BOTH reviewers independently refused the authority the contract first claimed.
  - (what changed) §11 said an escalation presents "at least two distinct, conflicting paths, without anchoring: no preferred option, no recommendation". It now requires a recommendation WITH its reasoning, alongside paths stated honestly enough that the CPO could pick the one not recommended. Anchoring stays forbidden in the narrow sense: never shade the alternatives to make the recommendation look inevitable, never guess what the CPO wants to hear.
  - (why it was escalated, having first NOT been) The contract initially recorded this as "one prose correction" resting on the plan's ExitPlanMode approval. The scope-auditor and the cto-reviewer each independently ruled that insufficient, and both were right: approving a plan that mentions a rule change is not a ruling on the rule, and §10 reserves "rule reinterpretation or extension" to the CPO with no exception for rules that are already stale. The cto-reviewer added the sharper objection — that a rule being changed to match the agent's own non-compliance is not a justification at all. Correct, and the reason the 9-to-8 split below is recorded as EVIDENCE OF STALENESS ONLY, never as the authority.
  - (evidence the written rule was stale, not the reason to change it) `escalations.log` at the time held 9 entries that withheld a recommendation per §11 and 8 that gave one. The CPO's memory file already said "lead with the decision and a bolded recommendation", and he asked for and acted on a recommendation twice inside this session. So the written rule contradicted both the CPO's stated preference and half of actual practice.
  - (how it was put) Two conflicting paths, no anchoring: (a) answer now, record the answer here, keep the change in this PR; (b) cut the rule change and its `CLAUDE.md` line out of this PR, leave the old rule standing, handle it separately later. Recommended (a), on the grounds that it costs one sentence and that (b) leaves `CLAUDE.md` pointing at a rule both parties intend to change; (b) named as better only if the CPO wanted time to think.
      CPO ANSWER (conversation, 2026-07-22): **"go ahead as recommended"** — path (a). §11 now requires a recommendation. `CLAUDE.md` line 19, which summarised §11 as "no recommendation" and is the first file read every session, is corrected in the same commit. This entry is the durable authority the contract's round-3 amendment rests on.

2026-07-22 (no branch — CPO ruling, banked for the next task) — DESERVED-VS-ACTUAL IS DOMESTIC-LEAGUE ONLY. Tournaments deferred.
  - (the defect, found while verifying #804) `deserved_rank` ranks ALL teams in a competition-season 1..N, but `latest_rank` for a group-stage tournament is position WITHIN a group (1..4). Not comparable. Every tournament team therefore carries a large false gap: AFCON 2025 sums to 299 deserved against 63 actual, roughly −10 per team, which reads as spectacular over-performance and means nothing. Domestic leagues are exact (Eredivisie 171 vs 171, gap 0). The model's coverage gate assumed knockout competitions carry no standing and would drop out on their own; group-stage tournaments DO carry standings, just not comparable ones, so the gate checks a position EXISTS rather than that it is the same KIND of number.
      CPO ANSWER (conversation, 2026-07-22): **"of course it is. We keep it for ranking in domestic leagues. It should be possible to have deserved vs actual for tournaments as well but let's skip for now."**
  - (what this settles) The read is DOMESTIC-LEAGUE ONLY. A tournament-appropriate version is wanted eventually and is explicitly NOT being designed now.
  - (what is still OWED, and it is not zero) The mart TODAY still emits non-null `deserved_rank` / `sot_rank_gap` for tournaments — misleading values sitting in `mart_team_profile`. Nothing displays them, because no page exists, so the risk is contained rather than live. Restricting the model to domestic leagues is owed work; until it lands, **the hero must never render this block for a non-domestic competition.**

