ewritten after every reviewer has passed with no gate noticing — whether to bind it is a mechanism question.
2026-08-01 fix/868-review-scope — FOUR CPO RULINGS on the review machinery itself. Recorded here because `contract.md` does not survive the next task and all three round-1 reviewers caught their absence. Blast radius: every future commit in this repo.
  - (why) #370 took TWELVE review rounds for a ~300-line change. The code was correct from round 5; rounds 6-12 found nothing a visitor to the site would see. Three mechanisms compounded: governance artifacts sat inside `scope_paths`, so reviewers read the paperwork the review itself produces (measured: 839 lines of code inside a 38,932-line reviewed diff); two evidence artifacts sat inside `diff_sha256`, so correcting a typo voided every PASS already given; and every brief required two named risks for a PASS, so on correct code a reviewer was OBLIGED to find something, and what it found was prose. He directed the measurement and I ran it, on the instrument built for exactly this claim (`scripts/report_process_health.py`): **CPO rulings per branch roughly 1.3 before the 2026-07-31 org change against 7.3 after**, when his stated goal was less interruption. ⚠ The report printed `1.24` and `10.50` and both were wrong in the direction that flattered the argument for changing the process — the baseline understated because four non-branch header keys inflate its denominator, and the "after" figure overstated because the #370 entry's header was mis-parsed into a phantom branch. Both defects are recorded in the banked note below; the jump is real, and smaller than first reported.
      CPO ANSWER (2026-08-01), shown the defect in plain language and asked change by change:
      (1) + (2) **"yes to those two"** — reviewers stop seeing the task notes, and fixing a note no longer restarts the review.
      (3) **"why would we force it? Of course, the reviewer needs to have he critical attitude but it's allowed to approve and not invent some finding."**
      (4) **"go ahead"** — implement all three.
  - (the CPO's correction of my diagnosis, and it stands as the ruling on the org) I had relayed an outside recommendation to cut the reviewers from six to three on the basis of low activation. He rejected it: **"What could be wrong with setting up an organization with roles that are activated when necessary? Maybe you just set it up completely wrong."** He was right and the repo's own numbers say so — `data-engineer-reviewer` fires on 1% of commits and caught 2 real bugs; `football-analytics-expert-reviewer` fires on 9% and caught 4; `scope-auditor` fires on 100% and produced ~25 of 28 paperwork findings. **A specialist that sleeps until its domain is touched is the cheapest thing in the system. Low activation is not a defect; it is the design working.** 7 of 8 reviewers are wired and fire correctly; only `seo-expert-reviewer` has no routing row. **THE ORG IS NOT THE PROBLEM AND DOES NOT CHANGE.** No role, brief, routing row or decision right was removed.
  - (⭐ the standing rule this produced, and it is general) **"if one comment doesn't pass the PR then you change it and only the new version stays. not the old and the new."** A correction REPLACES; it never accumulates. Applies to code comments, contracts, evidence artifacts and issue bodies. This was the single generator of #370's cost: every round appended "this said X until round N, which was wrong, now Y", keeping both — and that text was itself inside the reviewed diff, so the next round found defects in it. Measured: 134 of 220 added comment lines in `strings.ts` narrated the review rather than explaining the code. Applying the rule took #370's paperwork from ~3.5 MB to ~240 KB and its contract from 643 lines to 171.
  - (banked — WHAT ROUND 1 OF THIS BRANCH PROVED) All three reviewers FAILED it, and **not one finding was about artifact phrasing.** Every one was a real machinery defect: the CI twin still enforcing the old floor, a new code path failing open in the unsafe direction, two stale rule statements in guard files, and two unpinned tests. That is the evidence the change works — the reviewers went back to finding bugs. Keep the diff free of the review's own paperwork and adversarial review pays for itself again.
  - (banked) **The most serious round-1 finding was a verification I claimed and had not done.** `impact_map` said the PASS floor "exists in one place. Verified before editing." It existed in two, and the second was fail-closed CI on every PR, so change 3 was inoperative exactly where it mattered. An `impact_map` asserted from memory is worse than an absent one, because the reviewers are entitled to rely on it. Paste the evidence or do not make the claim.
